.TH EGRAPH 1 2026-09-29 "egraph @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
egraph \- query the dependency graph of the installed packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B egraph
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ command " [" command-options ] " [" arguments ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B egraph
keeps the dependency graph of a Gentoo system in a store file and answers questions about it in
milliseconds: what depends on a package, why it is installed, who links a soname, what is broken,
what
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove and what
.B emerge \-u
would merge.
.PP
The store is built by
.BR egraph-build (1),
which evaluates the installed packages through portage.
Every command first checks that the store still describes the system and brings it up to date
when it does not, unless
.B \-\-no\-refresh
says otherwise.
The answers are held to portage's own on generated systems and on a live one.
.PP
Without a command,
.B egraph
opens its interactive interface on a terminal (the same as
.BR "egraph tui" ),
and reads commands one per line otherwise (the same as
.BR "egraph shell" ).
.PP
Packages are named by installed cpv
.RI ( dev-libs/openssl-3.5.0 )
or cp
.RI ( dev-libs/openssl ,
every installed version); atoms take portage's syntax.
.SH OPTIONS
These come before the command.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the options, or a command's, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
The root whose installed packages to query (default
.BR $ROOT ,
else
.IR / ).
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-root " dir"
The root of the portage configuration to evaluate them with (default
.BR $PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT ).
.TP
.BI \-\-eprefix " dir"
The offset prefix of a prefix installation (default
.BR $PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX ).
.TP
.BI \-\-store " file"
The store to read (default
.BR $EGRAPH_STORE ,
else the system store; see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.BI \-\-builder " command"
The
.B egraph-build
that refreshes the store (default
.BR $EGRAPH_BUILD ,
else the one next to
.BR egraph ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.BI \-\-emerge " command"
The
.B emerge
that
.B \-\-verify
runs (default
.BR $EGRAPH_EMERGE ,
else the one in
.BR PATH ).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-refresh
Answer from a stale store instead of bringing it up to date.
.TP
.BR \-\-layout " auto|human|lines"
Results for people, or as tab-separated lines for scripts (default
.BR $EGRAPH_LAYOUT ,
else
.BR auto :
for people on a terminal).
.TP
.BR \-\-color " auto|always|never"
Colour the layout for people (default
.BR auto :
on a terminal, unless
.B NO_COLOR
is set).
.TP
.BR \-\-glyphs " nerd|unicode|ascii"
Icons in the layout for people: a Nerd Font's, plain Unicode, or ASCII (default
.BR $EGRAPH_GLYPHS ,
else
.B nerd
in a UTF-8 locale and
.B ascii
otherwise).
.SH COMMANDS
Commands that read dependencies take
.BR \-\-dynamic\-deps " y|n" :
read an installed package's dependencies from its ebuild when the same version is still in its
repository, as emerge's option of the name does (default
.BR y ).
Those that follow what keeps a package take
.BR \-\-with\-bdeps " y|n" :
whether build-time dependencies keep packages, as emerge's option does (default
.BR y ).
.SS deps \fIpackage\fR...
What installed packages depend on, with the atom and the kinds of dependency for each.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-possible
Also what the ebuilds would add with USE flags toggled, with the flags.
.SS rdeps \fIpackage\fR...
What depends on installed packages.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-possible
Also what the ebuilds would depend on them with USE flags toggled, with the flags.
.SS why \fIatom\fR
The shortest chain of dependencies from a root set
.RI ( @selected ", " @system ", " @profile )
that keeps each installed package the atom matches, as depclean finds it.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-with\-bdeps
.SS match \fIatom\fR...
The installed packages each atom matches.
.TP
.B \-\-candidates
Match the installed cps' ebuilds instead
.RI ( cpv::repo ),
with the USE each would be built with now, masked ones included.
.SS soname \fIsoname\fR
The installed packages that need a soname
.RI ( libssl.so.3 ).
.TP
.B \-\-providers
The packages providing it instead.
.SS broken
Installed dependencies that nothing installed satisfies.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.SS blockers \fR[\fIpackage\fR ...]
Blockers
.RB ( ! atom
and
.BR !! atom)
in the installed packages' dependencies, each with the installed packages it blocks: of the
packages given, every blocker they hold and every blocker holding them back; without packages,
the blockers that block something installed.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.SS orphans
The installed packages
.B emerge \-\-depclean
would remove.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-with\-bdeps
.SS updates
The installed packages
.B emerge \-u
would replace or rebuild, and the new packages it would pull in, each with the USE it would be
built with as
.B emerge \-v
shows it. Then the installed packages it would uninstall for a blocker between them and a merge,
and the blockers it could not resolve, for which it refuses the whole plan (exit status 6). On
the running system's root, emerge only resolves a strong blocker
.RB ( !! atom)
by a replacement.
A merge whose dependency nothing satisfies, or an ebuild with invalid metadata, is passed over for
another version, as emerge backtracks; when what emerge must merge has none left, the plan ends
with the dependencies no visible version matches, and emerge refuses it too.
So it does a version emerge would select whose REQUIRED_USE its USE leaves unsatisfied, even one
it then passes over: the plan ends with the constraints left unmet, which a
.I package.use
change must meet.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.B \-\-held
Also the updates that installed dependents hold back, which of their atoms do, and the commands
that would get past them.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
In merge order, each with the places of the merges it waits for.
.TP
.B \-\-tree
Each merge under the root set and the packages it comes from.
.TP
.B \-\-world
Only the packages the root sets keep, as
.B emerge \-u @world
does.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
Every package in scope, not only the arguments and what their merges need, as
.B emerge \-\-deep
does.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
Also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-newuse
makes for changed USE or IUSE.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
Also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-changed\-use
makes for changed USE.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Also run
.B emerge \-\-pretend
on the same request, under the same roots and without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
and show where its merge list differs: a package only one of them merges or uninstalls, a
different kind of merge, a new package's USE, or a blocker only one of them cannot resolve. The
differences go to standard error in the lines layout, and make the exit status 5.
.SS plan \fItarget\fR...
What
.B emerge \-\-pretend
would merge for the targets, in the lines
.B updates
prints: atoms (a name without its category takes the one the repositories know it in) and the sets
.BR @world ", " @selected ", " @system ", " @profile " and " @installed .
A package the request names is merged even when installed, as plain
.B emerge
does. A package only the repositories know is evaluated first by
.BR egraph\-build " \-\-evaluate"
(not with
.BR \-\-no\-refresh ),
and stays in the store until
.BR "egraph rebuild" .
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-update
Update each installed slot a target matches instead, and add its best version's slot, as
.B emerge \-u
does.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-deep
With
.BR \-u ,
every package in scope, as
.B emerge \-\-deep
does.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-noreplace
Skip a target something installed matches, as
.B emerge \-\-noreplace
does.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-newuse
With
.BR \-u ,
also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-newuse
makes.
.TP
.BR \-U ", " \-\-changed\-use
With
.BR \-u ,
also the rebuilds
.B emerge \-\-changed\-use
makes.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-table
In merge order, each with the places of the merges it waits for.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Also run
.B emerge \-\-pretend
on the same request, under the same roots and without
.BR EMERGE_DEFAULT_OPTS ,
and show where its merge list differs: a package only one of them merges, a different kind of
merge, or a new package's USE. The differences go to standard error in the lines layout, and
make the exit status 5.
.SS export \fR[\fIpackage\fR...]
Part of the graph, or all of it without packages, for other tools.
.TP
.BR \-\-format " dot|json"
Graphviz or JSON (default
.BR dot ).
.TP
.B \-\-evaluated
The evaluated store instead, whole and as JSON: the dependencies emerge reads by default, and the
versions each installed package could move to.
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " n"
Dependency edges to follow out from the packages (default 1).
.TP
.BR \-\-direction " reverse|forward|both"
Which edges to follow (default
.BR reverse ).
.SS stats
Store and graph statistics.
.SS tui
The interactive interface: the installed packages, pending updates, orphans and broken ones, each
package's page, the plan, the running emerges, and a
.B :
prompt taking these commands. It keeps the store current while it is open.
.SS shell
Commands read one per line from standard input, the stores loaded once; the global options that
choose the stores are taken from the command line only.
.SS rebuild
Rebuild the store from scratch.
.SS refresh
Bring the store up to date if what it was built from changed, printing nothing.
.SS check
Compare the store with a fresh build, exiting 4 when they differ.
.SS affected
What a set of merges can affect, as JSON, for portage's neighborhood completion.
.TP
.B \-\-dynamic\-deps
.TP
.BI \-\-request " file"
The JSON request (default
.BR \- :
standard input).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR ROOT ", " PORTAGE_CONFIGROOT ", " PORTAGE_OVERRIDE_EPREFIX
Defaults for
.BR \-\-root ", " \-\-config\-root " and " \-\-eprefix .
.TP
.BR EGRAPH_STORE ", " EGRAPH_BUILD ", " EGRAPH_EMERGE ", " EGRAPH_LAYOUT ", " EGRAPH_GLYPHS
Defaults for
.BR \-\-store ", " \-\-builder ", " \-\-emerge ", " \-\-layout " and " \-\-glyphs .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set and not empty, no colour unless
.B \-\-color always
asks for it.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Where the user's own store goes (default
.IR ~/.cache ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${ROOT}${EPREFIX}/var/cache/egraph/installed.egraph
The system store, with the evaluated store beside it; written by root.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CACHE_HOME}/egraph/installed.egraph
The store of a user who cannot write the system store, used while the system store is not
current.
.TP
.I /etc/portage/postsync.d/egraph
Refreshes the store after a sync.
.TP
.I /etc/portage/post_emerge.d/egraph
Refreshes the store after an emerge that changed the installed packages, once the dispatcher is
linked in place.
.TP
.I /usr/share/egraph/post_emerge
The dispatcher that runs every hook in
.IR /etc/portage/post_emerge.d .
portage runs only
.I /etc/portage/bin/post_emerge
after an emerge, which is the user's own file; without one, link this there.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure, such as a package that is not installed or a store that could not be built.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed.
.TP
.B 3
The command is not in this build:
.B tui
without Notcurses.
.TP
.B 4
.B check
found the store differing from a fresh build.
.TP
.B 5
.B \-\-verify
found emerge merging otherwise.
.TP
.B 6
emerge would refuse the plan of
.BR updates " or " plan :
it has blockers emerge cannot resolve, dependencies nothing satisfies, or REQUIRED_USE its USE
leaves unsatisfied.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B egraph rdeps dev\-libs/openssl
What depends on openssl, and through which atom.
.TP
.B egraph why net\-misc/networkmanager
Why networkmanager is installed.
.TP
.B egraph updates \-DN \-\-tree
What
.B emerge \-uDN @installed
would merge, under what keeps it.
.TP
.B egraph \-\-layout lines orphans
What depclean would remove, one cpv a line, for a script.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR egraph-build (1),
.BR emerge (1),
.BR portage (5)
